/* greatest common divisor, Vahid style */
while (1) {
  while (!go_i);      /* wait for start */
  x = x_i;  y = y_i;  /* load operands  */
  while (x != y) {
    if (x < y) y = y - x;
    else       x = x - y;
  }
  d_o = x;            /* output result  */
}

questions.questionText:
The program below is converted to an FSMD with Vahid's templates: every assignment gets its own state, and each while loop, including the outer while (1), gets a condition state plus a join state. How many states does the FSMD have, and how many flip-flops does a binary-encoded state register need?

while (1) {
  while (!start);
  sum = 0;
  i = 0;
  while (i < 8) {
    sum = sum + x_i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  s_o = sum;
}

The C loop below ends with sum = 10. A designer merges the two loop-body assignments into one FSMD state, where both registers are updated at the same clock edge from their values at the start of the state. With i and sum both starting at 0, what does the hardware produce?

i = 0; sum = 0;
while (i < 4) {
  i = i + 1;
  sum = sum + i;
}

A custom processor is built from the program below with Vahid and Givargis's one-to-one rules: one register for each variable and one for the output s_o (n_i is an input port, not a register), one functional unit for each arithmetic or comparison operation (no sharing), and a 2-to-1 multiplexer in front of any register written from two sources. What does the datapath contain?

sum = 0; i = 0;
while (i < n_i) { sum = sum + i; i = i + 1; }
s_o = sum;

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. x and y are signals of type unsigned(7 downto 0) initialised to 5 and 9. What are x and y after two rising edges of clk?

process (clk)
begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    x <= y;
    y <= x + 1;
  end if;
end process;

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. x, y and d_o are unsigned(7 downto 0), x_i = 20, y_i = 8, and st starts in S_LOAD. The case statement below is inside 'if rising_edge(clk) then'. After how many rising edges does st first equal S_DONE, and what is d_o then?

case st is
  when S_LOAD => x <= x_i; y <= y_i; st <= S_CMP;
  when S_CMP  => if x < y then st <= S_SUBY;
                 elsif x > y then st <= S_SUBX;
                 else st <= S_OUT; end if;
  when S_SUBY => y <= y - x; st <= S_CMP;
  when S_SUBX => x <= x - y; st <= S_CMP;
  when S_OUT  => d_o <= x; st <= S_DONE;
  when S_DONE => null;
end case;

